{
  "image_name": "1caa6d2930d8cd686fe73adaec94a178.png",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes how ER-positive metastatic breast cancer can evolve under treatment pressure over time. It starts with an ER-positive tumor mass, then shows therapy selecting different resistant subclones rather than producing one uniform outcome. The central message is that treatment drives tumor evolution along several possible paths, some of which keep reliance on estrogen receptor alpha (ERα) signaling, while others lose that dependency.\n\nOne branch retains ERα activity and leads to AI-resistant, ERα-dependent tumors, suggesting these cancers may still respond to stronger next-generation anti-ERα drugs such as oral SERDs. A second route involves transcription factor alterations, labeled with factors such as FOXA1, MYC, and CTCF; these tumors appear molecularly altered but their exact ERα dependence is shown as uncertain. Other branches reflect loss of ERα dependence, including tumors with increased RTK or MAPK pathway activity, such as HER2–EGFR–NF1-altered tumors, which are linked to anti-HER2 or MAPK-targeted therapy. A final path shows loss of ERα and luminal lineage factors, producing lineage-plastic tumors with unclear functional dependencies.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes heterogeneous resistance and multiple evolutionary escape routes during endocrine treatment.",
